<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Marquisate of Bodonitsa</h1><p class="st-meta">1204 CE – 1414 CE · capital Bodonitsa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Frankish frontier march guarding the pass of Thermopylae, which outlived most crusader states in Greece until the Ottomans took it in 1414.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1204 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface of Montferrat</a> granted the strategic castle of Bodonitsa, above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> pass, to the Italian knight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guido-pallavicini">Guido Pallavicini</a>. The marquisate was intended as a buffer protecting the Frankish lands of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> from attack from the north.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Bodonitsa was a small feudal state centred on its hill fortress, whose ruins still stand. Its marquesses were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> first of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-thessalonica">Kingdom of Thessalonica</a> and later of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea</a>, with some ties to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a>. The Pallavicini line was followed in 1335 by the Venetian Zorzi family through marriage, which brought Venetian protection.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Catalan, Byzantine and Turkish pressure reduced the marquisate. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> briefly took it in 1393 and finally captured Bodonitsa in 1414, ending the state. The Zorzi family kept the title in exile.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/marquisate-of-bodonitsa.jpg" alt="Marquisate of Bodonitsa" width="320" height="210"><figcaption>Image: Mark Landon (photographed in 1990; digitized in 2021) · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bodonitsa_castle_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1204 CE – 1414 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bodonitsa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Guido Pallavicini, Nicholas I Zorzi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Marquisate of Boudonitza, Bodonitza</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1309&amp;focus=marquisate-of-bodonitsa" aria-label="Marquisate of Bodonitsa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5241 787 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Marquisate of Bodonitsa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5561 1043L5563 982M5561 1043L5530 1071M5561 1043L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5563 982h0M5530 1071h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5561 1043h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5561 1043h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1309&amp;focus=marquisate-of-bodonitsa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

